  \n            \n            \n                \n                    \n                        \n        Article\n    \n        \n            Open access\n        \n    \n    \n\n                        Published: 07 May 2021\n                    \n\n                    Loss of function mutations in GEMIN5 cause a neurodevelopmental disorder\n                    Sukhleen Kour \n            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-8068-52781 na1, Deepa S. Rajan1 na1, Tyler R. Fortuna1, Eric N. Anderson1, Caroline Ward1, Youngha Lee2, Sangmoon Lee2, Yong Beom Shin3, Jong-Hee Chae4, Murim Choi \n            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-9195-14552,4, Karine Siquier5, Vincent Cantagrel \n            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-5180-48485, Jeanne Amiel6, Elliot S. Stolerman7, Sarah S. Barnett8, Margot A. Cousin \n            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-6119-93469, Diana Castro10, Kimberly McDonald11, Brian Kirmse12, Andrea H. Nemeth13,14, Dhivyaa Rajasundaram15, A. Micheil Innes \n            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0001-9881-546716, Danielle Lynch16, Patrick Frosk \n            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-9673-795X17, Abigail Collins18, Melissa Gibbons18, Michele Yang18, Isabelle Desguerre19, Nathalie Boddaert20, Cyril Gitiaux21, Siri Lynne Rydning \n            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0001-9584-032522, Kaja K. Selmer23, Roser Urreizti \n            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0003-3617-713424, Alberto Garcia-Oguiza25, Andrés Nascimento Osorio26, Edgard Verdura27, Aurora Pujol \n            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-9606-060027,28, Hannah R. McCurry29, John E. Landers30, Sameer Agnihotri31, E. Corina Andriescu32, Shade B. Moody32, Chanika Phornphutkul33, Maria J. Guillen Sacoto34, Amber Begtrup \n            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-2872-328134, Henry Houlden \n            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-2866-777735, Janbernd Kirschner36, David Schorling36, Sabine Rudnik-Schöneborn37, Tim M. Strom38, Steffen Leiz39, Kali Juliette40, Randal Richardson40, Ying Yang41, Yuehua Zhang41, Minghui Wang42, Jia Wang43, Xiaodong Wang \n            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-5374-023543, Konrad Platzer44, Sandra Donkervoort45, Carsten G. Bönnemann45, Matias Wagner \n            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-4454-882346, Mahmoud Y. Issa \n            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-3899-282147, Hasnaa M. Elbendary47, Valentina Stanley48, Reza Maroofian35, Joseph G. Gleeson \n            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-0889-922048, Maha S. Zaki \n            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0001-7840-000247, Jan Senderek49 & …Udai Bhan Pandey \n            ORCID: orcid.org/0000-0002-6267-01791,50,51 Show authors\n\n                    \n\n                    \n                        \n    Nature Communications\n\n                        volume 12,  Article number: 2558 (2021) Cite this article\n                    \n                    \n        \n        \n            \n                \n                    \n                        22k Accesses\n                    \n                \n                \n                    \n                        51 Citations\n                    \n                \n                \n                    \n                        \n                            129 Altmetric\n                        \n                    \n                \n                \n                    \n                        Metrics details\n                    \n                \n            \n        \n    \n                    \n                \n\n                \n    \n        Subjects\n        \n            Developmental disordersNeurodevelopmental disorders\n        \n    \n\n                \n    \n    \n\n    \n    \n\n                \n\n            \n\n        \n            AbstractGEMIN5, an RNA-binding protein is essential for assembly of the survival motor neuron (SMN) protein complex and facilitates the formation of small nuclear ribonucleoproteins (snRNPs), the building blocks of spliceosomes. Here, we have identified 30 affected individuals from 22 unrelated families presenting with developmental delay, hypotonia, and cerebellar ataxia harboring biallelic variants in the GEMIN5 gene. Mutations in GEMIN5 perturb the subcellular distribution, stability, and expression of GEMIN5 protein and its interacting partners in patient iPSC-derived neurons, suggesting a potential loss-of-function mechanism. GEMIN5 mutations result in disruption of snRNP complex assembly formation in patient iPSC neurons. Furthermore, knock down of rigor mortis, the fly homolog of human GEMIN5, leads to developmental defects, motor dysfunction, and a reduced lifespan. Interestingly, we observed that GEMIN5 variants disrupt a distinct set of transcripts and pathways as compared to SMA patient neurons, suggesting different molecular pathomechanisms. These findings collectively provide evidence that pathogenic variants in GEMIN5 perturb physiological functions and result in a neurodevelopmental delay and ataxia syndrome.\n\n            \n                \n            \n\n            \n                \n                    \n        \n            \n                Similar content being viewed by others\n                \n                    \n                        \n                            \n                                \n                                    \n                                \n                                \n                                    \n                                        Structural basis for Gemin5 decamer-mediated mRNA binding\n                                        \n                                    \n                                    \n                                        Article\n                                         Open access\n                                         02 September 2022\n                                    \n                                \n                            \n                        \n                    \n                        \n                            \n                